// ora-mod-ai — generated by tools/merge.js: src/ora-mod-ai-*.js plus matching questions from the Oracle role banks (tools/modules.js).
QB['ora-mod-ai'] = (QB['ora-mod-ai'] || []).concat([["AI","How would you architect AI agents in an Oracle Finance landscape responsibly?","- Use **Oracle AI Agent Studio** for agents that operate on Fusion data with Fusion security.\n- **Use cases** with clear ROI: invoice exceptions, collections emails, reconciliation explanations, close insights.\n- **Guardrails**: agents act within the user's or a service role's privileges; **human approval** for financial postings and payments.\n- **Auditability**: log prompts, actions and decisions.\n- **Data privacy**: no sensitive data to external models without approval.\n- **Measure**: accuracy, time saved, exceptions.\n- **Change management** for finance users.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","Explain the architecture of an AI agent in Oracle AI Agent Studio.","- **Agent**: instructions + topics + tools + an LLM, running natively in Fusion on OCI.\n- **Tools** connect the agent to data and actions: Business Object (Fusion APIs), Document (RAG over documents, including external repositories), External REST/Connector, MCP, Email, Deep Link, User Session.\n- **Workflows**: nodes (business object, code, email trigger, vector DB read/write, human approval, agent nodes) for deterministic, auditable processes; can be chained.\n- **Security**: identity propagation — agents act with the user's Fusion roles; a **credential store** holds keys for external services.\n- **Observability**: monitoring dashboard (sessions, latency, errors, tokens), tracing, evaluations.\n- **Interoperability**: invoked from Fusion pages, Teams, external apps; **A2A** and **MCP** for cross-vendor agents and tools.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","When would you use a workflow agent instead of a free-form conversational agent?","- **Workflow** when steps are known, outcomes must be consistent and auditable (invoice exception routing, accrual preparation, supplier onboarding checks). Add **agent nodes** only where judgment/interpretation is needed.\n- **Conversational** for open questions and help (policy Q&A, status inquiries).\n- Finance generally favors workflows with **human approval nodes** for anything that changes financial data.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","How do RAG and document tools work in AI Agent Studio, and what should you watch for?","- **Document tool / vector DB nodes**: documents (policies, contracts, procedures) are chunked and embedded; the agent retrieves relevant pieces to ground answers. Oracle supports multimodal content and external repositories (e.g. SharePoint).\n- **Watch for**: document quality and versioning (stale policies), access control on documents, citing sources/deep links, testing with tricky questions, and refreshing indexes when documents change.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","How would you connect a Fusion agent to non-Oracle systems?","- **External REST / Connector tools** for systems with APIs (bank portals, tax engines, CRM).\n- **MCP tools** where the external system exposes an MCP server.\n- **A2A** to collaborate with agents on other platforms.\n- **OIC** behind the scenes for complex orchestration and transformations.\n- **Credential store** for secrets; least-privilege service accounts; monitoring of failures.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","How do you choose the LLM for an agent?","- Oracle provides **Fusion-optimized models**, and you can choose premium models from several providers (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, Meta, Cohere, xAI as announced).\n- Choose by **task**: reasoning-heavy exceptions vs simple lookups; latency; cost (token usage for premium models); data/residency requirements.\n- **Evaluate** candidates with the same evaluation set before deciding; re-evaluate when models change.","D"],["Redwood","Explain the Redwood extension architecture vs classic Fusion customization.","- **Classic (ADF)** pages: Page Composer/sandboxes, Application Composer in some areas, Groovy.\n- **Redwood** pages: built on Oracle JET/Visual Builder; extended with **Visual Builder Studio** — page extensions (fields, layouts, rules in JavaScript-based events), stored in Git, deployed via pipeline.\n- **Flexfields** remain the data extension mechanism.\n- **APIs/FBDI unchanged**; deep-link formats can change.\n- Architects should plan the **retirement of classic personalizations** and avoid fragile CSS/DOM tweaks — use Redwood design tokens and supported extension points.","D"],["Redwood","How do you build a custom Redwood-style application for Finance users?","- **Visual Builder Studio** to create a Redwood app using Fusion REST APIs (Business Objects) and Redwood components — looks native.\n- Host as an extension or standalone VB app; SSO with Fusion identity.\n- Use for gaps like a custom reconciliation workbench or a guided intake form.\n- Include in the extension inventory, CI/CD and regression testing.","D"],["Fusion Platform","What changed in Fusion integration and data options recently that an architect should know?","- **Business events** + OIC for event-driven integration.\n- **REST APIs** expanding across modules; FBDI remains for bulk.\n- **BICC** for bulk extracts; **Fusion Data Intelligence** as the analytics platform.\n- **AI Agent Studio** tools reuse the same business objects/APIs.\n- **Redwood** page extensions via VBS.\nDesign new integrations around APIs and events; keep FBDI for volume.","D"],["AI","Where would you apply AI agents in a Fusion SCM landscape?","- **Procurement**: supplier onboarding agent, requisition assistant, contract risk review, PO change negotiation drafts.\n- **Order management**: exception triage (holds, late lines), customer communications.\n- **Planning**: forecast anomaly explanations, planner assistant for exceptions.\n- **Inventory/quality**: anomaly detection in adjustments and nonconformances.\n- **Product data**: enrichment and classification.\n\nGuardrails: role-based access, human approval for commitments (POs, prices), auditing of agent actions.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","Design an agentic architecture for order exception management in Fusion SCM.","- **Events**: order holds, scheduling failures, late shipments (business events / scheduled checks).\n- **Workflow agent**: Business Object nodes gather order, supply (GOP), shipment and customer data → agent node classifies the exception and proposes actions (reschedule, split, substitute, expedite).\n- **Human approval** for customer-impacting changes; Email/Deep Link tools notify owners.\n- **External tools**: carrier tracking via REST/MCP; WMS status via connector.\n- **Monitoring**: exception volumes, resolution time, agent accuracy.\n- **Security**: identity propagation and least-privilege tools.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","How would you use MCP and A2A in a supply chain landscape?","- **MCP tools** let Fusion agents call tools exposed by other systems (WMS, TMS, supplier portals, planning tools) through a standard protocol.\n- **A2A** lets a Fusion agent collaborate with agents on other platforms (e.g. a logistics provider's agent for tracking or rebooking).\n- Architect: trust boundaries, credentials in the credential store, logging, rate limits, and clear ownership of each agent.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","What makes a supply chain agent safe to deploy at scale?","- **Deterministic workflows** for core steps; agents only where judgment is needed.\n- **Draft-then-approve** for commitments (POs, order changes).\n- **Evaluation sets** including edge cases and \"must refuse\" requests.\n- **Tracing** and dashboards; alerting on error spikes.\n- **Gradual rollout** by BU/site.\n- **Fallback** to manual process when the agent or LLM is unavailable.","D"],["Redwood","How do Redwood and Visual Builder Studio change your extension strategy for SCM?","- Classic personalizations (Page Composer) will retire with classic pages — **rebuild only what's valuable** in VBS.\n- Prefer **configuration and rules** in VBS over custom pages.\n- For bigger needs (e.g. a dock scheduling screen), build a **Redwood app in Visual Builder** on Fusion REST APIs.\n- Treat VBS assets as code (Git, reviews, pipeline, regression each quarter).","D"],["Redwood","What should you test when SCM pages switch to Redwood?","- End-to-end flows: requisition → PO → receipt → invoice; order → ship → bill; transfers; counts.\n- **Mobile/tablet** use on the warehouse floor and **barcode scanning** behavior.\n- **Printing** (labels, documents) and **notifications/deep links**.\n- **VBS extensions** and rules.\n- **Performance** for high-volume users.\n- Reports embedded on pages.","D"],["Fusion Platform","How do you design integrations for Fusion SCM using business events?","- Subscribe in **OIC** to events like PO approved, shipment confirmed, receipt created, item released.\n- Push to downstream systems (supplier portal, WMS, e-commerce, analytics) in near real time.\n- **Idempotency** (events can repeat), ordering where needed, error queues and replay.\n- Combine with REST lookups for full payloads.","D"],["Redwood","How would you plan your team's work for Oracle's move from classic to Redwood pages?","- **Inventory** affected pages and personalizations per module; estimate rebuild effort in VBS.\n- **Plan waves** by module aligned to Oracle's retirement dates, avoiding close.\n- **Assign owners**; build VBS skills in the team.\n- **Regression testing** and user training in each wave.\n- Report progress and risks to the Director and business owners.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","Your CFO asks: \"What should we do with Oracle AI Agent Studio?\" How do you respond?","- Explain briefly: Oracle's tool to use delivered agents and build our own inside Fusion, with our security.\n- Propose **2–3 pilots** with clear value (AP inquiries/holds, expense policy help, collections emails).\n- **Controls**: human approval for financial actions; audit involvement.\n- **Measure** and report in 90 days.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","How would you staff and run an AI agent pilot?","- Small team: a functional lead, a technical consultant (tools/APIs), a business owner, and a tester.\n- Scope one process; define success metrics.\n- Build, evaluate with real scenarios, pilot with 5–10 users.\n- Weekly review; decide scale/stop at the end.","B"],["AI","What is your AI strategy for the ERP platform?","- **Use embedded Oracle AI** first (agents, IDR, predictions) — lower cost and risk.\n- **Prioritize use cases** with measurable value: AP processing, collections, close insights, procurement assistance.\n- **Governance**: risk assessment, human-in-the-loop for financial actions, auditability, security.\n- **Pilots** with metrics; scale successful ones.\n- **Upskill** team on AI Agent Studio and prompt design.\n- **Change management** for users.","D"],["AI","How would you govern AI agents acting in Oracle ERP?","- **Approval process** for each agent use case (business owner, security, compliance).\n- **Least-privilege** roles for agents; actions logged.\n- **Human-in-the-loop** for financial commitments.\n- **Monitoring**: accuracy, exceptions, drift.\n- **Incident handling** for wrong actions.\n- **Policy** aligned with corporate AI governance.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","What is your strategy for AI agents on the Oracle ERP platform?","- **Start with Oracle-delivered agents** and AI Agent Studio (native security, lower risk).\n- **Use-case portfolio** across AP, close, procurement, order management, HR — scored by value and risk.\n- **Governance**: risk tiers, human approval for financial/commitment actions, evaluations, monitoring, audit involvement.\n- **Skills**: build a small agent team in the ERP CoE.\n- **Measure** value (cycle times, productivity) and cost (premium LLM tokens).","D"],["AI Agent Studio","How do you decide between Oracle AI Agent Studio and a general enterprise AI platform?","- **Oracle AI Agent Studio** for processes centered on Fusion data — inherits Fusion security, uses business objects, delivered templates, Oracle supports it.\n- **Enterprise AI platform** for cross-system assistants and non-ERP use cases.\n- Connect them via **A2A/MCP** and APIs instead of duplicating.\n- Decide per use case; avoid building ERP agents outside Fusion without a reason.","D"],["Redwood","How do you fund and govern the Redwood transition?","- Treat it as a **program** with a budget: VBS rebuilds, testing, training.\n- Use it to **simplify**: retire unneeded personalizations.\n- **Timeline** driven by Oracle's classic retirement dates.\n- **Governance**: change control for VBS extensions; regression automation.","D"],["Fusion Basics","What is the difference between Oracle E-Business Suite and Oracle Fusion Cloud ERP?","- **EBS** is on-premise (or hosted) — the customer owns the database, applies patches and can customize code directly. Forms-based UI, concurrent programs, responsibilities.\n- **Fusion Cloud ERP** is SaaS — Oracle hosts and upgrades it quarterly (e.g. 25A, 25B). No direct database access; you extend through configuration, Visual Builder, OIC, BI Publisher and REST APIs. Browser UI, scheduled processes, role-based security.\n\n## What changes for a consultant\n- Setups are done through **Functional Setup Manager (FSM)** tasks instead of individual forms.\n- You must regression-test every quarterly update.\n- Reporting uses OTBI, BI Publisher and Financial Reporting/Smart View.\n\nTip: If you've worked on both, say which and give one example of how you did the same task in each.","B"],["Fusion Basics","What is Functional Setup Manager and how do you use it?","FSM is the Fusion tool for all application setup.\n\n- **Offerings** (e.g. Financials) contain **functional areas** (e.g. Payables), which contain **setup tasks** (e.g. Manage Payables Options).\n- You **opt in** to the offerings and features you need, then work through the task list.\n- **Implementation projects** group tasks and let you assign them to team members and track status.\n- **Configuration packages** export setups from one environment and import them into another (e.g. TEST → PROD).\n- **Scope values** let you set up a task for a specific BU or ledger.\n\nTip: Mention you used configuration packages to move setups between pods — it shows you know the deployment process.","B"],["Redwood","What is Oracle Redwood and how is it different from the classic Fusion pages?","**Redwood** is Oracle's new user experience and design system for Fusion Applications.\n- **Classic pages** are built on older ADF technology — dense forms, many tabs, the \"Alta\" look.\n- **Redwood pages** are rebuilt: cleaner layouts, guided flows, smart defaults, better search, mobile-friendly, and AI assistance built in (summaries, suggestions).\n- Oracle is moving modules to Redwood release by release and **retiring the classic pages** over time — so consultants need to know both during the transition.\n- Redwood pages are extended with **Visual Builder Studio**, not Page Composer.\n\nTip: Mention which Redwood pages you have used (e.g. Redwood Payables invoice pages, Manage Journals, Expenses).","B"],["Redwood","How do you turn on a Redwood page in Fusion?","- Many Redwood pages are enabled with **profile options** (Setup and Maintenance → Manage Administrator Profile Values), usually named for the page, set at site level.\n- Some are delivered as **opt-in features** in Functional Setup Manager; newer ones become the default automatically in a later release.\n- Always enable in a **test environment first**, run your test scripts, check personalizations and reports, and train users before switching production.\n\nTip: Say you check the quarterly **readiness (\"What's New\")** documents to see which pages became Redwood.","B"],["Redwood","A user says a field they always used is missing on the new Redwood page. What do you do?","- Check whether the field moved (Redwood often groups fields differently or puts them in a drawer or \"Show more\").\n- Check if it was a **personalization** on the classic page (Page Composer) — those don't carry over to Redwood.\n- If needed, add it back using **Visual Builder Studio** (show the field, make it required, default a value) — done by a consultant/admin with the right role, tested in a sandbox/test pod first.\n- Confirm the field is still populated by integrations and reports.","B"],["AI Agent Studio","What is Oracle AI Agent Studio?","**AI Agent Studio** is the tool inside Oracle Fusion Applications for **creating, configuring, testing and managing AI agents**.\n- Oracle delivers **ready-made agents and templates** (e.g. for payables, procurement, HR questions) that you can use or adjust.\n- You can **build your own** agents with instructions, \"topics\" and **tools** that let the agent read or act on Fusion data (business objects), documents, email and outside systems.\n- Agents **respect Fusion security** — they act with the user's roles and data access.\n- You can choose the **LLM** behind an agent and monitor usage and quality.\n\nTip: A junior consultant isn't expected to be an expert — show you know what it is and one use case in your area.","B"],["AI Agent Studio","What is the difference between an AI agent and a simple chatbot?","- A **chatbot** answers questions from a fixed script or a knowledge base.\n- An **AI agent** can **reason about a goal and take actions**: look up an invoice, check a PO, draft an email, create or update a record — using tools, with rules about what it may do.\n- In Fusion, agents run inside the application's security and can include **human approval** before important actions.\n\nExample: \"Why is this invoice on hold?\" — an agent can read the holds, the PO and receipts, explain the cause and suggest the next step.","B"],["AI Agent Studio","Give an example of how an AI agent could help an AP team.","- **Invoice question agent**: suppliers or internal users ask \"When will invoice 123 be paid?\" — the agent reads invoice status, holds and the next payment run and answers.\n- **Hold resolution helper**: explains why an invoice is on hold and who needs to act.\n- **Invoice intake**: reads emailed invoices (with IDR) and prepares them for review.\n- People still **approve** payments and changes — the agent saves time on lookups and routine steps.","B"],["Redwood","What is Oracle Redwood and what changes for SCM users?","**Redwood** is Oracle's new user experience for Fusion Applications, replacing the older ADF-based \"classic\" pages module by module.\n- Cleaner, guided pages (e.g. Redwood **requisitions**, **purchase orders**, **receipts**, **inventory transactions**, **Product Management** item pages).\n- Better search and mobile use; AI help such as summaries and suggestions.\n- Classic personalizations don't carry over; Redwood pages are extended with **Visual Builder Studio**.\n- Users need short training because screens and navigation change.","B"],["Redwood","How would you prepare warehouse users for a switch to Redwood receiving or inventory pages?","- Enable the Redwood page in **test** first; run receiving/transfer scenarios.\n- Note differences: field locations, scanning flow, defaults.\n- Rebuild any needed personalizations with Visual Builder Studio.\n- Create a **one-page job aid** with screenshots; train super users first.\n- Switch production at a quiet time (not month-end or a peak shipping day) with floor support.","B"],["Redwood","What is Visual Builder Studio used for in Redwood?","**Visual Builder Studio (VBS)** is how you extend and adjust Redwood pages:\n- Show/hide or reorder fields, make fields required or read-only, set defaults.\n- Add **rules** (e.g. show a field only for a certain item type).\n- Add custom fields (flexfields) to the page.\n- Changes are made in a workspace, previewed, then **published** — usually by an admin or technical consultant.\nClassic **Page Composer** is for classic pages only.","B"],["AI Agent Studio","Give an SCM example where an AI agent helps users.","- **Requisition assistant**: an employee says \"I need 5 laptops for new hires\" — the agent finds the catalog item, fills the requisition and submits for approval.\n- **Order status agent**: customer service asks \"Where is order 5521?\" — the agent checks the order, shipment and tracking and answers.\n- **Supplier quote agent**: reads an emailed supplier quote and drafts the requisition or PO lines for a buyer to review.\nPeople approve purchases and commitments; the agent removes lookups and typing.","B"],["AI Agent Studio","What does \"the agent follows Fusion security\" mean in practice?","- The agent runs with the **identity and roles** of the person using it (or a defined service role).\n- If a buyer can only see POs for their BU, the agent only sees those too.\n- Actions like creating a requisition go through the **same approvals** as when a person does it.\n- This keeps data access and controls (SOX, SoD) intact.","D"],["Quarterly Updates","How do you manage HCM quarterly updates, especially payroll?","- Review **What's New** for HCM and **payroll legislative updates** (tax changes).\n- **Regression**: core HR transactions, approvals, security, payroll runs compared against prior results (automated payroll comparison).\n- **Statutory patches** (year-end) planned and tested.\n- Extracts and integrations re-tested.\n- Communication to HR users.","B"],["AI","How is AI being used in Oracle HCM?","- **Generative AI**: job descriptions, performance review summaries, goal suggestions, candidate outreach.\n- **AI agents**: HR help desk answers (policies), benefits questions, onboarding assistance.\n- **Recommendations**: skills-based job/learning recommendations, best candidates.\n- **Guardrails**: bias checks, privacy, human review for decisions (hiring, ratings).","D"],["Redwood","What changes for HCM users and admins with Redwood?","- HCM was one of the first areas moved to **Redwood**: Person, Employment, Journeys, Goals, Performance, Learning, Recruiting pages.\n- **Transaction design**: Redwood uses **Transaction Design Studio** and **Visual Builder Studio** to control fields, sections and rules by action/role — classic Page Composer personalizations don't carry over.\n- **Approvals** and security are unchanged.\n- Plan training for managers and HR because self-service screens look different.","D"],["Redwood","How would you hide or require a field on a Redwood HCM action like Promote?","- Use **Transaction Design Studio** (or VBS for Redwood pages) to create a rule for the **action** (Promote), optionally by legal employer or role.\n- Set the field to **required/hidden/read-only**; add defaults.\n- Test with different users and countries; then publish.\n- Document the rule for support.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","Give examples of AI agents useful in HCM.","- **Policy/benefits Q&A agent** grounded on HR documents (Document tool).\n- **Employee self-service agent**: \"update my address\", \"how many vacation days do I have?\" using HCM business objects with the employee's own access.\n- **Manager assistant**: summarize team absences, upcoming reviews, drafts of performance feedback.\n- **Recruiting assistant**: draft job descriptions, summarize candidates.\nKeep humans responsible for hiring, pay and performance decisions.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","What extra care do HCM agents need?","- **Sensitive data** (salary, health, national IDs) — strict role-based access; agents inherit HCM security.\n- **Bias and fairness** in recruiting/performance uses — human decisions, testing for bias.\n- **Privacy/GDPR** and works council requirements in some countries.\n- **Clear disclosure** to employees that they're using an AI assistant.","D"],["Fusion Platform","How do you keep HCM extracts and integrations safe during Redwood changes?","- HCM Extracts, HDL and APIs are not affected by UI changes, but **new fields** added through Redwood/VBS flexfields must be added to extracts.\n- Re-test **approval notifications** and deep links.\n- Regression test payroll-related transactions after each quarterly update.","B"],["AI","How are AI and agents being used in Oracle Fusion Financials?","- **Intelligent Document Recognition** for invoices.\n- **AI agents** (Oracle AI Agent Studio): e.g. payables agents that process invoices, ledger agents for close insights, collections agents drafting customer communications.\n- **Generative AI** in narratives (e.g. variance explanations), supplier communications, and help.\n- **Predictive**: payment time predictions, anomaly detection in journals, expense auditing.\n- Configuration via AI Agent Studio with guardrails and human approval.\n\nTip: Show practical judgment — where AI helps (high-volume, rules-based) and where controls still need humans.","D"],["Quarterly Updates","How do you set up governance for continuous Oracle Cloud releases?","- A **release management** process: review readiness docs each quarter, assess impact, decide on opt-ins.\n- **Regression automation** (e.g. Oracle's testing tools or third-party like Opkey/Worksoft) for core flows.\n- **Change Advisory Board** to approve configuration changes and new features.\n- **Roadmap** of features to adopt aligned to business priorities.\n- Communicate changes to users with short release notes.","B"],["Redwood","You lead the Finance workstream and Oracle is retiring the classic pages you use. How do you plan it?","- **Track Oracle's schedule** in the quarterly readiness material — which Finance pages become Redwood and when classic is retired.\n- **Impact assessment** by role: AP clerks, accountants, approvers, expense users.\n- **Personalization inventory** and a rebuild plan in **Visual Builder Studio** (keep only what matters).\n- **Regression test pack** updated for Redwood flows.\n- **Change management**: super users, short videos, \"what moved where\" guides.\n- **Sequence** by module outside close; measure tickets and cycle times after each switch.","D"],["Redwood","What governance would you put around Visual Builder Studio changes?","- Treat VBS changes like code: **workspaces in a Git repository**, peer review, testing in a test pod, and controlled **publish** by authorized admins.\n- Naming standards and documentation of each extension.\n- **SOX change management** evidence for controlled pages (approvals, testing).\n- Regression on every quarterly update.\n- Limit who has Visual Builder administrator roles.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","How would you design an AI agent for AP hold resolution?","- **Goal**: cut time invoices sit on hold.\n- **Workflow**: triggered for new holds → **Business Object tools** read the invoice, PO, receipts, tolerances → **agent node** explains the cause and recommends an action (e.g. \"receive remaining 20 units\", \"price differs from agreement by 4%\") → routes to the right person (buyer, requester) with an **Email tool** and **Deep Link**.\n- **Human in the loop**: people release holds or change POs.\n- **Measure**: days on hold, holds per invoice, AP touches.\n- **Pilot** with top suppliers; evaluate accuracy; scale.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","How do you decide whether to use a delivered Oracle agent, a partner agent or build your own?","- **Delivered agents/templates first** — supported by Oracle, updated with releases.\n- **Marketplace (partner) agents** — when a validated partner agent fits a specific need.\n- **Build your own** — when the process is specific to your company or combines your data and policies.\n- Criteria: fit, maintenance effort, security, cost (premium LLM token usage), and how each affects controls.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","How do you keep finance controls intact when introducing AI agents?","- Agents inherit **Fusion roles and data access**; never give broader service access than needed.\n- **No autonomous postings/payments** — human approval nodes for anything financial.\n- **Audit trail**: traces and logs kept; agent actions identifiable.\n- **SoD**: agent actions count under the user who invoked them.\n- **Evaluations** and periodic review of accuracy.\n- Involve **internal audit** early; document the control design.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","How would you measure the value of AI agents in Finance?","- **Before/after** baselines: cycle time (invoice processing, hold resolution, close tasks), touches per transaction, tickets/questions handled, errors.\n- **Usage** and adoption from the monitoring dashboard.\n- **Quality**: accuracy from evaluation sets and user feedback.\n- **Cost**: token usage for premium LLMs vs time saved.\nReport monthly to the controller.","D"],["AI","Where can AI help in Oracle SCM today?","- **Procurement**: AI agents for supplier onboarding, requisition assistance, contract analysis; spend classification.\n- **Planning**: machine-learning demand forecasting, supply chain risk alerts.\n- **Order management**: order exception handling agents; generative AI for customer communications.\n- **Inventory**: anomaly detection in adjustments.\n- **Product Hub**: AI-generated item descriptions and attribute enrichment.\n\nApproach: start with high-volume, rules-based tasks with human review.","D"],["Redwood","How do you lead a Redwood transition across Procurement, Inventory and Order Management?","- **Roadmap** from Oracle's readiness schedule: which SCM pages move when, and when classic pages are retired.\n- **Impact by persona**: requesters, buyers, receivers, warehouse staff, order administrators.\n- **Personalizations** inventory → rebuild in **Visual Builder Studio** only where valuable.\n- **Testing** of end-to-end flows plus mobile/scanning use on the floor.\n- **Training** on the floor with super users; avoid peak periods.\n- **Hypercare** after each switch.","D"],["Redwood","What new capabilities in Redwood SCM pages would you highlight to the business?","- **Guided buying** and smarter search in requisitions.\n- **Redwood purchase order and agreement** pages with clearer status and actions.\n- **Redwood receiving/inventory** pages that are easier on tablets and mobile.\n- **Product Management** NIR and change orders with better collaboration.\n- **AI assistance**: summaries, suggested values, generated descriptions.\n- Fewer clicks for common tasks.","B"],["AI Agent Studio","Design an AI agent workflow for supplier delivery delays.","- **Trigger**: scheduled daily or on supplier ASN/acknowledgment changes.\n- **Business Object tools**: find PO schedules at risk (past promised date, no ASN), check affected sales/work orders.\n- **Agent node**: assess impact and draft a supplier follow-up email and an internal summary.\n- **Human approval node**: buyer reviews/edits and sends (Email tool).\n- **Updates**: buyer records new promise dates; planners see impact.\n- **Measure**: on-time delivery, expedites, buyer time.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","How would you use an AI agent with Product Management (item data)?","- **Item creation assistant**: proposes item class, attributes and description from a request or supplier spec sheet (Document tool).\n- **Data quality agent**: finds duplicates or missing attributes and suggests fixes.\n- **NIR helper**: summarizes pending NIRs and what's blocking them.\n- Keep **approvals** in the NIR workflow; agents propose, people approve.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","What risks do AI agents bring to supply chain processes and how do you manage them?","- **Wrong actions** (ordering wrong items/quantities) → draft-only, human approval before commitments.\n- **Data exposure** → Fusion security inheritance, least-privilege tools.\n- **Hallucinated answers** to customers → ground answers in business objects; evaluation sets; show sources/deep links.\n- **Cost** → monitor token usage for premium LLMs.\n- **Change** → train users to review outputs critically.","D"],["Quarterly Updates","How do you manage Oracle's quarterly updates?","- Read the **release readiness** content and **What's New** documents for the modules.\n- Identify **opt-in features** worth enabling and mandatory changes.\n- Updates go to TEST two weeks before PROD: run a **regression test pack** (ideally automated) for key flows: P2P, O2C, close, payments, integrations, reports.\n- Log issues, raise SRs early.\n- Communicate changes to users (release notes, training).","B"],["Redwood","How do you plan the move from classic to Redwood pages for a Finance module?","- **Inventory**: which pages users use (AP invoices, journals, expenses, receipts) and which are now Redwood in the current release.\n- **Personalizations**: find classic Page Composer changes (the Personalization helper tools list them) — they don't migrate; decide which to rebuild in **Visual Builder Studio**.\n- **Test**: enable Redwood pages via profile options/opt-in in a test pod; run test scripts end to end, including reports and deep links in emails.\n- **Train**: short role-based sessions and job aids.\n- **Switch** module by module outside month-end; monitor tickets.\nOracle is retiring classic pages over coming releases, so plan rather than wait.","D"],["Redwood","How would you add a required field and a validation to a Redwood Payables page?","- Create a **descriptive flexfield** segment (if it's new data) and deploy it.\n- In **Visual Builder Studio**, open the Payables page extension in a workspace.\n- Add the field to the layout; set **required** and a **rule** (e.g. required only when invoice type = Standard and amount > $10k).\n- **Preview**, test in the test pod, then **publish**.\n- Update integrations (FBDI/REST) and reports to include the new field.","D"],["Redwood","What happens to OTBI reports, BIP reports and integrations when pages move to Redwood?","- **REST/SOAP APIs, FBDI and the data model don't change** — integrations generally keep working.\n- **OTBI/BIP** reports keep working; embedded analytics on pages may look or behave differently — test them.\n- **Deep links** (e.g. in approval emails or reports) can change format — test notification links.\n- **Personalizations** must be rebuilt in VBS.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","Walk me through creating an AI agent in AI Agent Studio.","- Start from a **delivered template** or the **agent builder assistant** (describe what you want in plain words) — or build from scratch.\n- Write clear **instructions** (role, goal, boundaries, tone).\n- Add **topics** to scope what it handles.\n- Add **tools**: e.g. a **Business Object tool** to read invoices and holds, a **Document tool** for policy documents, an **Email tool**, a **Deep Link tool** to open the record in Fusion.\n- Choose the **LLM**.\n- **Test** in the studio with sample questions; review the trace of tool calls.\n- **Publish** and make it available to the right roles/pages.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","What tool types can an agent use in AI Agent Studio?","- **Business Object tool** — read or act on Fusion objects through APIs (invoices, suppliers, POs, journals).\n- **Document tool** — answer from uploaded documents (policies, procedures) using retrieval (RAG).\n- **External REST tool / Connector tool** — call outside systems.\n- **MCP tool** — connect to tools exposed through the Model Context Protocol.\n- **Email tool** — send emails.\n- **Deep Link tool** — give the user a link straight to the Fusion page.\n- **User session tool** — use context about the signed-in user.\nChoose the minimum tools the agent needs.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","How would you test an agent before giving it to AP users?","- Build an **evaluation set**: 20–50 realistic questions with expected answers (holds, payment dates, policy questions, edge cases, things it must refuse).\n- Run it in the studio; review **traces** (which tools were called, what data came back).\n- Check **security**: test with users of different BUs to confirm data limits.\n- Check **tone and accuracy**; tighten instructions.\n- Pilot with a few users; monitor the **dashboard** (sessions, errors, latency, token use).","D"],["AI Agent Studio","Which Finance processes are good candidates for AI agents, and which are not?","## Good candidates\n- High-volume questions and lookups: invoice/payment status, policy questions, expense help.\n- Exception triage: explaining holds, suggesting fixes, drafting supplier/customer emails.\n- Document intake: reading invoices, remittances, receipts.\n- Reconciliation help: suggesting matches with explanations.\n## Poor candidates (without strong controls)\n- Approving payments, posting journals, changing bank details — keep **human approval**.\n- Judgment-heavy accounting decisions.","D"],["Quarterly Updates","How do you approach quarterly update testing for SCM?","- Review **What's New** for Procurement, Inventory, OM, Costing, Product Hub.\n- Run an **automated regression** pack for P2P, O2C, transfers, cost close and key integrations (OIC, B2B).\n- Check custom reports (BIP/OTBI), Visual Builder extensions and **REST API** changes.\n- Test opt-in features in a sandbox before enabling.\n- Plan a quick smoke test after the PROD update weekend.","B"],["Redwood","How do you roll out Redwood pages for Procurement and Inventory?","- List the **classic pages in use** and which now have Redwood versions (requisitions, POs, receipts, transfers, item pages).\n- Find **personalizations** on classic pages (helper tools list them); decide which to rebuild in **Visual Builder Studio**.\n- Enable Redwood in **test** (profile options / opt-ins), run P2P and inventory scenarios, check reports, notifications and deep links.\n- Train buyers, requesters and warehouse users with short role-based guides.\n- Switch by area, away from month-end and peak shipping; watch tickets closely for two weeks.","D"],["Redwood","How would you add a rule on the Redwood requisition page — for example, require a justification above $5,000?","- In **Visual Builder Studio**, open the requisition page extension in a workspace.\n- Use the justification field (or add a flexfield).\n- Add a **rule**: required when requisition amount > 5,000 (or by category).\n- Preview with test data, then **publish** after testing in the test pod.\n- Consider whether this belongs in **approval rules** instead (who approves) vs page validation (what must be entered).","D"],["Redwood","What is the Redwood Product Management experience and how does it affect item creation?","- Redwood pages for **items, item classes, New Item Requests and change orders** in Product Management (Product Hub).\n- Guided item creation by item class with templates and required attributes.\n- **NIR workflows** with definition and approval steps assigned to teams.\n- Better search and AI assistance (e.g. generated descriptions).\n- Extend pages through Visual Builder Studio; existing classic personalizations must be rebuilt.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","How would you build an agent that turns supplier quote emails into requisitions?","- **Trigger**: an **Email trigger** or inbound mailbox in an agent workflow.\n- **Read**: a **Document tool** / file processing to extract supplier, items, quantities, prices from the PDF.\n- **Match**: **Business Object tool** to find the supplier, catalog items or agreements.\n- **Act**: create a **draft requisition** (Business Object tool) — not submit.\n- **Human approval node**: buyer reviews and submits.\n- **Notify**: Email tool with a **Deep Link** to the draft.\n- Test with real quotes; monitor errors and accuracy.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","What is the difference between a workflow agent and a hierarchical (supervisor) agent?","- **Workflow agent**: a defined sequence of **nodes** (business object, code, email trigger, human approval, agent nodes). Predictable and auditable — best for business-critical processes.\n- **Hierarchical/supervisor**: a supervisor agent decides which specialist agents to call — more flexible, less predictable.\n- Oracle has been moving toward **workflows**, with agent nodes inside them for steps that need judgment.\n- Choose workflows when the steps are known and controls matter.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","How do you control what an SCM agent is allowed to do?","- **Security**: agent uses the user's roles/data access (identity propagation) — no extra access.\n- **Tools**: give only the tools needed; read-only where possible.\n- **Instructions**: clear boundaries (\"never submit a PO; create drafts only\").\n- **Human approval nodes** before commitments.\n- **Monitoring**: traces and dashboards; review exceptions.\n- **Evaluations** with \"must refuse\" test cases.","D"],["AI","How would you lead an enterprise AI program for back-office processes?","- **Vision**: augment teams, automate routine work, improve insight.\n- **Portfolio** of use cases across finance, procurement, supply chain, HR — scored by value and risk.\n- **Platforms**: embedded AI in Oracle first; enterprise AI platform for cross-system use.\n- **Governance**: responsible AI policy, risk tiers, human oversight, audit trails.\n- **Data foundations**.\n- **Workforce impact** planning and reskilling.\n- **Value tracking**.","D"],["AI","How do you address workforce concerns about AI replacing jobs in finance and operations?","- **Transparent communication** on goals: augment and redeploy capacity to higher-value work.\n- **Involve** teams in designing AI use cases.\n- **Reskilling** programs (analysis, exception handling, AI supervision).\n- **Measure** and share outcomes honestly.\n- Work with HR on workforce planning.","D"],["AI","What risks do you see with AI in enterprise applications and how do you mitigate them?","- **Accuracy/hallucination** → human review, grounding in enterprise data, testing.\n- **Security/privacy** → data access controls, no sensitive data leakage, vendor assessments.\n- **Compliance/auditability** → logging, explainability.\n- **Bias** in HR use cases → testing and oversight.\n- **Over-reliance** → process controls.\n- **Cost** → usage monitoring.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","How would you scale AI agents across ERP, HCM and SCM portfolios?","- **Enterprise agent operating model**: intake, risk tiers, standards, evaluation, monitoring, ownership.\n- **Platform choices**: Oracle AI Agent Studio for Fusion-centric agents; enterprise AI platform for cross-domain; interoperability via A2A/MCP.\n- **Value portfolio** with finance validation.\n- **Workforce plan**: reskill teams in finance, procurement, HR operations.\n- **Board/audit** reporting on AI risk and value.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","How do you think about the workforce impact of agents in finance and procurement operations?","- Agents take over **lookups, routine drafting and triage**; people focus on exceptions, judgment, supplier/customer relationships.\n- **Plan capacity** redeployment with HR (not just cuts).\n- **Reskill**: exception handling, data analysis, agent supervision.\n- Communicate openly to keep trust.","D"],["Redwood","Why does Redwood matter strategically, beyond new screens?","- It's the **foundation for Oracle's AI features** — new capabilities ship on Redwood pages first.\n- **Classic retirement** makes it mandatory; delaying increases risk and cost.\n- Chance to **simplify** customizations and improve user productivity.\n- Plan it as part of the platform roadmap, not as IT housekeeping.","B"],["AI Agent Studio","How would you set up an enterprise operating model for AI agents on Oracle Fusion?","- **Intake and prioritization** of agent use cases with business owners (value, risk, feasibility).\n- **Risk tiers**: informational agents (low), drafting/assisting (medium), agents that change records (high — human approval mandatory).\n- **Standards**: instruction templates, tool usage rules, naming, evaluation-set requirements, monitoring thresholds.\n- **Lifecycle**: build → evaluate → pilot → publish → monitor → re-evaluate on model/release changes.\n- **Ownership**: each agent has a business owner and a technical owner.\n- **Audit**: control documentation, traces retained, periodic review.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","How do you evaluate and monitor agent quality over time?","- **Evaluation sets** per agent: correctness, safety (\"must refuse\"), tone, tool-use correctness; run before each publish and after Oracle updates or LLM changes.\n- **Monitoring dashboard**: sessions, latency, errors, token usage; alert on drift.\n- **Tracing** to debug wrong answers (which tool returned what).\n- **User feedback** loops.\n- **Quarterly review** with owners; retire agents that don't deliver value.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","An agent gave a supplier the wrong payment date. How do you investigate?","- Pull the **trace** for that session: question, tools called, data returned, model output.\n- Was the **data** wrong (payment schedule changed after), the **tool** wrong (filtered on the wrong field), or the **model** misread it?\n- Fix accordingly: tool definition, instructions (\"only quote dates from the scheduled payment record\"), or add a **deep link** and caveat.\n- Add the case to the **evaluation set**; re-run.\n- Communicate with the supplier and AP.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","What is your view on letting agents post journals or release payments?","- Not autonomously in most organizations today. Agents can **prepare** (draft journals with support, recommend payment holds/releases), but posting and payment release stay with authorized humans via **approval nodes**.\n- Revisit for low-risk, high-volume cases once accuracy is proven, controls are designed, and audit agrees (e.g. auto-clearing small reconciliation differences within policy).\n- Document the decision in the control framework.","D"],["Redwood","How would you lead an enterprise-wide Redwood adoption program?","- **Roadmap** aligned with Oracle's classic retirement schedule; module waves.\n- **Extension rationalization**: retire most personalizations; rebuild essentials in VBS with standards.\n- **Regression automation** updated for Redwood.\n- **Change management** at scale (role-based learning, super users).\n- **Metrics**: adoption, ticket volumes, task times.\n- **Engage Oracle** on gaps and bugs early.","D"],["Fusion Platform","How do you keep a Fusion Finance platform current with quarterly releases, Redwood and AI features?","- **Release governance**: assess every quarter (mandatory changes, Redwood pages, new AI features).\n- **Opt-in decisions** with business owners; adoption roadmap.\n- **Automated regression** (UI and API).\n- **Sandbox** for evaluating new features and agents.\n- **Communication** to users each release.\n- Track value of adopted features.","B"],["AI","How would you lead AI adoption in supply chain operations?","- **Use-case portfolio** with value and feasibility: forecast enhancement, PO exception handling, supplier communication, order exception triage, quality anomaly detection.\n- **Data readiness** first.\n- **Pilot** with clear metrics and human-in-the-loop.\n- **Governance**: approvals, auditability, model monitoring.\n- **Change management**: planners and buyers as co-designers.\n- **Scale** what works; retire what doesn't.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","How would you build a roadmap of AI agents for a global supply chain?","- **Wave 1 (assist)**: order and PO status agents, policy/procedure Q&A, item description generation.\n- **Wave 2 (prepare)**: supplier quote/email intake to draft requisitions, order exception triage, supplier delay follow-up drafts.\n- **Wave 3 (act with guardrails)**: automated rescheduling within rules, auto-creation of low-value POs under agreements, with monitoring and approval thresholds.\n- Each wave: value cases, evaluation, controls, change management.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","How do you govern agents across Fusion and other platforms (WMS, TMS, partners)?","- **Registry** of agents (owner, purpose, tools, data, risk tier).\n- **Interoperability standards**: MCP for tools, A2A for agent-to-agent, credential store for secrets.\n- **Security**: least privilege, identity propagation, logging across platforms.\n- **Monitoring** in one view where possible; incident process for agent errors.\n- **Contracts** with partners covering agent behavior and data use.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","An order exception agent is rescheduling lines incorrectly for one region. How do you approach it?","- **Pause** autonomous actions for that region (switch to draft/approval).\n- **Traces**: which data and rules led to the decision; compare with correct cases.\n- Typical causes: regional sourcing rules, calendars, time zones, units of measure, incomplete data in tools.\n- Fix tools/instructions/data; add regional cases to **evaluation sets**; re-enable gradually.","D"],["Redwood","How would you manage Redwood adoption across 30 warehouses and plants?","- **Pilot site** first with floor testing (devices, scanners, printers).\n- **Wave plan** avoiding peaks; site readiness checklists.\n- **Standard VBS extensions** for all sites; no site-specific variants without approval.\n- **Training kits** and site champions.\n- **Monitoring** of task times and errors after each wave.","D"],["Fusion Platform","How do you balance adopting Oracle's new SCM AI features vs third-party tools?","- **Embedded first** when it covers the need — security, data and upgrades handled by Oracle.\n- **Third-party** when there is a clear capability gap (e.g. advanced optimization, specialized WMS AI).\n- **Evaluate** with real data and the same KPIs.\n- Consider integration cost, data duplication and lock-in.","B"],["AI","How do you build an AI-ready enterprise systems organization?","- **Skills**: AI agent design, prompt engineering, data engineering, model governance.\n- **Roles**: AI product owners, AI platform team, responsible AI lead.\n- **Processes**: use-case intake, risk review, testing, monitoring.\n- **Partnerships**: vendors (Oracle, hyperscalers), academia.\n- **Culture**: experimentation with guardrails.","D"],["AI","How would you measure the value of AI in back-office operations?","- **Productivity**: transactions per FTE, hours saved, capacity redeployed.\n- **Cycle time**: invoice processing, close, order exceptions.\n- **Quality**: error rates, exception rates.\n- **Financial**: cost reduction, working capital improvements.\n- **Experience**: employee and supplier/customer satisfaction.\n- **Risk**: control effectiveness.\nBaseline before deployment; attribute carefully.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","How would you explain Oracle AI Agent Studio to the board?","- \"Oracle now lets us run **AI agents inside our ERP**, using our existing security, to handle routine work like invoice questions, exception triage and procurement requests.\"\n- **Value**: faster processes, lower cost per transaction, better service.\n- **Controls**: humans approve financial actions; everything is logged.\n- **Plan**: pilots → scale, with measured results and risk oversight.","D"],["AI Agent Studio","What metrics would you report to the executive team on AI agents?","- **Adoption**: active users, sessions.\n- **Productivity**: hours saved, cycle times, automation rates.\n- **Quality**: accuracy, escalations, incidents.\n- **Cost**: LLM/token spend vs savings.\n- **Risk**: control exceptions, audit findings.","D"],["Redwood","How do you position the Redwood transition with executives?","- **Mandatory** (classic pages are being retired) — plan and fund it.\n- **Opportunity**: better user productivity, foundation for AI features, simplified customizations.\n- **Risk** of delay: rushed changes, disruption at close/peak times.","B"]]);
